diagnosis_text:
A thorough four-phase sweep of the simple_bgp topology found no fault matching the reported symptom. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) returned zero flagged devices: no ACL rules, no static-ARP injection, no duplicate MACs, all interfaces UP with baseline-only carrier counters, and no addressing anomalies on either host. Phase B (bgp_snapshot + tc_snapshot) showed both FRR BGP sessions in Established state with standard 180 s hold / 60 s keepalive timers, connections established = 1 / dropped = 0 on both routers, and zero BGP Notifications exchanged; the tc_snapshot found only the default fq_codel qdisc on all six interfaces — no netem delay, no tbf rate-limiter, no injected packet loss. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, no resource exhaustion, and no suspect processes on any device. A direct inspection of the FRR debug log confirmed the BGP session was established once at startup and has exchanged only regular keepalives since then — no session resets, no Notification messages, no withdrawn prefixes. Interface carrier counters on both inter-router and host-facing links are at zero, ruling out any link-flap history. A process scan found no background flap script, hping, iperf, or netem process that could produce periodic drops. End-to-end ping at the time of investigation showed 0% loss in both directions. In summary, no fault mechanism or historical trace consistent with intermittent packet loss exists in this topology at this time; the reported drops are unconfirmed and no corrective action is indicated.
